Function name.response:
Based on the CVE-2014-3610 vulnerability and the provided code blocks, the vulnerable functions are:

## Primary Vulnerable Functions:

1. **`wrmsr_interception`** (in `arch/x86/kvm/svm.c`)
2. **`handle_wrmsr`** (in `arch/x86/kvm/vmx.c`)

Both functions handle WRMSR (Write Model-Specific Register) instructions from guest VMs but failed to properly validate non-canonical addresses before writing to MSRs.

## Vulnerable Code:

**From `wrmsr_interception` (SVM):**
```c
static int wrmsr_interception(struct vcpu_svm *svm)
{
	struct msr_data msr;
	u32 ecx = svm->vcpu.arch.regs[VCPU_REGS_RCX];
	u64 data = (svm->vcpu.arch.regs[VCPU_REGS_RAX] & -1u)
		| ((u64)(svm->vcpu.arch.regs[VCPU_REGS_RDX] & -1u) << 32);

	msr.data = data;
	msr.index = ecx;
	msr.host_initiated = false;

	svm->next_rip = kvm_rip_read(&svm->vcpu) + 2;
	if (svm_set_msr(&svm->vcpu, &msr)) {
		trace_kvm_msr_write_ex(ecx, data);
		kvm_inject_gp(&svm->vcpu, 0);
	} else {
		trace_kvm_msr_write(ecx, data);
		skip_emulated_instruction(&svm->vcpu);
	}
	return 1;
}
```

**From `handle_wrmsr` (VMX):**
```c
static int handle_wrmsr(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
{
	struct msr_data msr;
	u32 ecx = vcpu->arch.regs[VCPU_REGS_RCX];
	u64 data = (vcpu->arch.regs[VCPU_REGS_RAX] & -1u)
		| ((u64)(vcpu->arch.regs[VCPU_REGS_RDX] & -1u) << 32);

	msr.data = data;
	msr.index = ecx;
	msr.host_initiated = false;
	if (vmx_set_msr(vcpu, &msr) != 0) {
		trace_kvm_msr_write_ex(ecx, data);
		kvm_inject_gp(vcpu, 0);
		return 1;
	}

	trace_kvm_msr_write(ecx, data);
	skip_emulated_instruction(vcpu);
	return 1;
}
```

## The Vulnerability:

Both functions extract MSR data from guest registers and pass it to their respective MSR setting functions (`svm_set_msr` or `vmx_set_msr`) without properly validating that the addresses are canonical. This allowed guests to write non-canonical addresses to host MSRs, potentially causing host system crashes (denial of service).

The vulnerability specifically affects the handling of guest WRMSR instructions in both AMD SVM and Intel VMX virtualization implementations within KVM.

