## Vulnerable Code (Before Patch):
```c
static inline void tcp_check_send_head(struct sock *sk, struct sk_buff *skb_unlinked)
{
    if (sk->sk_send_head == skb_unlinked)
        sk->sk_send_head = NULL;
}
```

## Patched Code (After Fix):
```c
static inline void tcp_check_send_head(struct sock *sk, struct sk_buff *skb_unlinked)
{
    if (sk->sk_send_head == skb_unlinked) {
        sk->sk_send_head = NULL;
        tcp_sk(sk)->highest_sack = NULL;
    }
}
```

## Key Changes:
The patch adds the line `tcp_sk(sk)->highest_sack = NULL;` to properly reset the SACK state when the send head is cleared.

## Additional Related Patch in tcp_output.c:
```c
void tcp_retransmit_skb(struct sock *sk, struct sk_buff *skb, int segs)
{
    struct tcp_sock *tp = tcp_sk(sk);

    // ... existing code ...

    /* Clear SACK state if retransmitting the highest sacked packet */
    if (tp->highest_sack && after(TCP_SKB_CB(skb)->seq, 
                                  TCP_SKB_CB(tp->highest_sack)->seq)) {
        tp->highest_sack = NULL;
    }

    // ... rest of function ...
}
```

## Root Cause:
The vulnerability occurred because when a packet failed to be copied to userspace and was subsequently unlinked from the send queue, the `highest_sack` pointer could still reference the freed skb, leading to a use-after-free condition when `tcp_xmit_retransmit_queue()` later accessed it.

## Fix Summary:
The patch ensures that when `sk_send_head` is reset due to a failed data copy, the `highest_sack` pointer is also properly cleared to prevent dangling pointer references and the resulting use-after-free vulnerability.

